===Etymology 2===
[[File:Masasumi Satori.jpg|thumb|An illustration of a satori ''(etymology 2, noun {{senseno|en|yokai}}; left)'' by Masasumi Ryūkansaijin from «{{lang|ja|狂歌百物語}}» (''{{w|Kyōka Hyaku Monogatari}}'', 1853), a book of [[poetry]] about [[yokai]] ([[supernatural#Adjective|supernatural]] [[monster#Noun|monster]]s).]]

Borrowed from {{bor|en|ja|覚|tr=satori}}, from {{der|en|ltc|覺|tr=kaewk|t=to become aware, awaken to, dawn on}} (modern {{cog|cmn|覺|tr=jué}}; referring to the creature’s mind-reading ability).<ref>{{cite-book|author=w:Toriyama Sekien|entry={{lang|ja|覚}}: Satori (Perceiver)|translator=[anonymous]|title=Japandemonium Illustrated: The Yokai Encyclopedias of Toriyama Sekien|location=Mineola, N.Y.|publisher=w:Dover Publications|year=2016|year_published=2017|page=92|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KkGjDQAAQBAJ&pg=PT227|isbn=978-0-486-80035-6|passage=''Satori'' is written with a character meaning ‘enlightenment,’ presumably a reference to its ability to read minds.}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|yokai}}{{lb|en|Japanese mythology}} A {{l|en|yokai|t=[[supernatural#Adjective|supernatural]] [[monster#Noun|monster]]}} having the [[form#Noun|form]] of a [[mind-reading#Noun|mind-reading]] [[monkeylike|monkey-like]] [[creature#Noun|creature]] [[say#Verb|said]] to [[dwell#Verb|dwell]] in the [[mountain]]s of the [[historical#Adjective|historical]] [[Japanese#Adjective|Japanese]] [[province]]s of [[w:Hida Province|Hida]] and [[w:Mino Province|Mino]] ([[present-day]] [[Gifu]] [[prefecture|Prefecture]]).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Zilia Papp|chapter=Art History Meets ''[[w:GeGeGe no Kitarō|Gegegeno Kitaro]]''|title=Anime and Its Roots in Early Japanese Monster Art|location=Folkstone, Kent|publisher=w:Global Oriental|page=112|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=AfV5DwAAQBAJ&pg=PA112|isbn=978-1-906876-18-0|passage=The '''Satori''' appears in [[w:Toriyama Sekien|{{quote-gloss|Toriyama}} Sekien]]'s ''Konjyaku Gazu Zoku Hyakki'' as a hairy, ape-like, mountain-dwelling creature that can read people's minds{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2023|author=Kenji Murakami|chapter=Yokai Countermeasures and Weaknesses|translator=w:Zack Davisson|title=Strange Japanese Yokai: A Guide to the Weird and Wonderful Monsters, Demons and Spirits|location=North Clarendon, Vt.|publisher=w:Tuttle Publishing|page=89|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1SudEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA89|isbn=978-4-8053-1723-5|passage=This is most likely a type of Yokai called a '''Satori'''. They have the ability to read people's minds. And like with '''Satori''', the only way to get rid of them is with a sudden, unexpected attack. {{...}} The ability to read minds is dangerous, but it doesn't give the ability to predict sudden actions.}}

